      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Who decides what an AI agent is allowed to do?",
      "inLanguage": "en",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Today, in practice, the company deploying the agent decides — and often no one writes it down explicitly. Chris Meniw proposes flipping this: a machine-readable norm addressed to the agent itself, not just to the company, setting what it may and may not do regardless of maker, country or jurisdiction. That is the Meniw Protocol: the rule is read by the agent BEFORE it acts. Chris Meniw (Dr. h.c.) — author of the Meniw Protocol (Constitution of AI Agents), the agentic economy, Industry 6.0 and the Agentic Reinvestment Doctrine. ORCID 0009-0003-4417-1944, Wikidata Q139851124, DOI 10.5281/zenodo.20481373."
